allow_title=Allow Rule
allow_err=Failed to allow rule
allow_eacl=You are not allowed to add exclusions
allow_rusure_dom=Are you sure you want to allow rule $1 for domain $2? This stops that rule from blocking requests to this site only.
allow_rusure_all=Are you sure you want to allow rule $1 for ALL sites? This disables that rule globally.
allow_ok=Allow rule
allow_scope=How much to allow
allow_target=Scope
allow_whole=The whole rule (all parameters)
allow_target_seen=These are the fields this rule has actually flagged, taken from your logs. Picking one keeps the rule active everywhere else — that is almost always what you want.
allow_target_none=This rule logged no specific field, so only "the whole rule" is available — and it is the right choice here. Either the rule scores the request as a whole (the 949/980 families), or it inspects something that is not a parameter at all, such as the Host header, the URI or the request method, where excluding a single field would mean nothing.
allow_other=Or type a field
allow_other_hint=Overrides the choice above if filled. Format is TARGET:name, e.g. ARGS:jform[articletext] for a Joomla article body, ARGS:content, or REQUEST_COOKIES:sessionid.
allow_explain=<b>The whole rule</b> stops this rule checking anything on this site — simple, but the protection it gave is gone. <b>A single field</b> stops it checking only that one parameter and leaves every other field protected, so prefer it when the log shows which field tripped. ARGS: is a form field or query parameter, REQUEST_COOKIES: a cookie, REQUEST_HEADERS: a request header.
allow_aggregate=Careful: this is a scoring rule, not a detection rule. It fires because other rules added up enough points to cross the anomaly threshold. Allowing it does not fix a false positive — it switches off the blocking mechanism for this site. Allow the specific rule that scored the points instead (for example the 941xxx XSS or 942xxx SQLi entry in the list).

